WEBVTT
1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.140
In the last lesson, we looked at how
to improve retrieval results

2
00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:05.740
by augmenting the query
we send with an LLM.

3
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:10.660
In this lesson, we're going to use a
technique called cross-encoder re-ranking to score

4
00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:14.420
the relevancy of our retrieved results
for the query that we sent.

5
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:15.660
Let's dig in.

6
00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:19.580
Re-ranking is a way to
order results and score

7
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:21.780
them according to their
relevancy to a particular

8
00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:22.400
query.

9
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.400
So let's take a look at
how this works underneath.

10
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.440
In re-ranking, after
you retrieve results for

11
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.320
a particular query, you
pass these results along with

12
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.080
your query to a re-ranking model.
This allows you to re-rank the output

13
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.440
so the most relevant
results have the highest

14
00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.280
rank. Another way to
think about this is

15
00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:42.040
your re-ranking model scores each
of the results conditioned on the

16
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.620
query, and those with the
highest score are the most

17
00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:45.260
relevant.

18
00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.280
Then you can just
select the top ranking

19
00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.920
results as the most
relevant to your particular query.

20
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.160
So let's take a look at how
to do this in practice.

21
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:54.900
First, we import our
helper functions as before.

22
00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:58.790
And we load the data into Chroma.

23
00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.610
So one use of re-ranking is to get more
information out of the

24
00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:06.090
long tail of query results.

25
00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:08.450
So let's
take a look at this query that we've already

26
00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.790
covered once before, which is
what has been the investment in

27
00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:13.190
research and development.
And usually we've been asking

28
00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:15.590
for five results in
return for our particular

29
00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:17.750
query, but now we're
gonna ask for 10.

30
00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:21.270
That means we're gonna get a longer tail
of possibly useful results. And

31
00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.930
again, we're gonna include documents and
embeddings. And so

32
00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:26.470
let's retrieve the
documents and taking a look at

33
00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:29.350
what we get, we see that
we get the same first five results as

34
00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:31.450
before because retrieval is
deterministic, but we also

35
00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.550
have five new results, which might have
relevant information to our

36
00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:37.430
question. The trick is
to figure out which of these

37
00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:40.710
results are actually relevant to our
specific query instead

38
00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.170
of just being
the nearest neighbors in embedding space.

39
00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:44.770
And the way
we do that is through

40
00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:47.310
using a cross-encoder re-ranking. So we're
going to use the sentence

41
00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:50.410
transformers cross-encoder, and we're
going to instantiate it

42
00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:51.430
with a particular
model.

43
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.620
So what is a cross-encoder model?

44
00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.480
Sentence transformers are made up
of two kinds of models. There's

45
00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.380
something called a Bi-encoder, where a
Bi-encoder encodes queries

46
00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.440
separately, and then we
can use the output of

47
00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.260
those Bi-encoders to perform cosine
similarity and find

48
00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.640
the nearest neighbors.
In contrast, a BERT

49
00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.120
cross-encoder takes both our query
and our document and passes it

50
00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.740
through a classifier which outputs a
score.

51
00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:18.419
And in this way,
we can use our cross-encoder

52
00:02:18.419 --> 00:02:20.444
to score our
retrieved results by

53
00:02:20.444 --> 00:02:23.898
passing our query and each retrieved
document and scoring

54
00:02:23.898 --> 00:02:28.067
them using the cross-encoder. We
can use the cross-encoder by passing

55
00:02:28.067 --> 00:02:31.997
in the original query and each
one of the retrieved documents and

56
00:02:31.997 --> 00:02:35.094
using the resulting
score as a relevancy or ranking

57
00:02:35.094 --> 00:02:37.060
score for our retrieved results.

58
00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:39.100
So we've
instantiated our cross encoder. And

59
00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.020
the first thing we're going
to do is create pairs.

60
00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.060
The pairs consist of
our query and each

61
00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:45.840
doc in our retrieved documents.

62
00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:50.560
And we're just going to ask the cross
encoder to score each pair.

63
00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:52.300
So let's print out our scores.

64
00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.160
And while we see that
the first two documents have

65
00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:57.760
high scores for our query,
we notice first of

66
00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:59.420
all that the second
retrieved document is actually

67
00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.740
a much higher score
than our first one. And also some

68
00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:04.320
documents in the longer tail of
retrieved results have

69
00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:07.240
higher scores than some of the
documents in the first five.

70
00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:10.680
So what would that look like if we
were to reorder our

71
00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:13.300
documents according to score?
We see that the second

72
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.240
document is now ranked first.

73
00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.480
First document is ranked second.
And something in the long

74
00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:19.600
tail actually makes it into
the top five and in

75
00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.780
fact the top five re-ranked
results contain results

76
00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:24.060
that are originally
the sixth and

77
00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.680
seventh results while
the fourth and fifth results are

78
00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:28.000
actually ranked quite low.

79
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.211
So in this way we've used the cross-encoder and
the score that it

80
00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:33.303
produces to re-rank our results,
and now if

81
00:03:33.303 --> 00:03:35.735
we were to cut to this
top five we'd see that the

82
00:03:35.735 --> 00:03:38.022
results should be much
more relevant than what

83
00:03:38.022 --> 00:03:40.260
we had before because
we've mined more of the

84
00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:43.520
long tail for information that's
actually relevant to our question.

85
00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.860
Now you might already see where I'm
going with this.

86
00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.540
But given the number
of results that we

87
00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.660
get with query expansion
and the way in

88
00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.440
which each generated query
addresses a different part

89
00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.640
of the complex question,
we can use the cross-encoder re-ranker technique

90
00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.580
to actually get
all of the best results for

91
00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:02.760
the original query from the augmented
expanded queries instead

92
00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.660
of just sending all of them
to the LLM. And here's how we

93
00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:06.100
do that.

94
00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:08.440
So from the previous lab,
this is just our original

95
00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:10.340
query and the generated queries.
And I've

96
00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:13.360
saved them into text here for you. And
then we do the same thing.

97
00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:15.780
We concatenate the
original and generated

98
00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:18.420
queries together and
we retrieve the results.

99
00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.980
And then, as last time,
we deduplicate the retrieved results.

100
00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:28.080
And now we create pairs,
just as we did in the previous example,

101
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.520
where we make pairs
of the original query

102
00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:33.720
and each retrieved document.
In this way, we can compute the

103
00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.600
relevancy of the retrieved
results for the augmented

104
00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.340
queries to the original
query and select among

105
00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:41.340
them the five best
that we actually want

106
00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:44.580
to pass to the LLM. So let's
create those pairs and let's score

107
00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:44.940
them.

108
00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:49.860
And one great thing about using a cross-encoder
model like this one is it's extremely

109
00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:51.600
lightweight and runs
completely locally.

110
00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:53.900
So here are the scores
of all of our retrieved results.

111
00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.520
And we can use these
scores to order our results

112
00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.040
and give us a new ordering.

113
00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.320
And then we can pass the
top five of these new results

114
00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:03.160
to the LLM and get the
most relevant information

115
00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:06.020
from this long tail of queries
that we got through query expansion

116
00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:08.920
and the retrieved results for our
augmented queries.

117
00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:11.640
So, in this lab,
we learned how to use a cross-encoder

118
00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:16.480
as a re-ranking model, and we've
seen how we can apply re-ranking both to get

119
00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:19.320
more out of the long tail of a
single query, as well as to

120
00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:23.100
filter the results of an augmented
expanded query to

121
00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:26.020
only the results
relevant to the original query itself.

122
00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:27.920
This is a really
powerful technique and worth

123
00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.800
experimenting with some more.
And it's a good

124
00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.220
idea to try to understand and get
an intuition for how the

125
00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:36.460
re-ranking score might change depending on
query, even when

126
00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:38.260
the result that your
retrieval system is giving

127
00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:41.140
you are the same.
This is because the cross-encoder re-ranker

128
00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:43.020
can emphasize different parts of
the query

129
00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:44.360
than the embedding model
does.

130
00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:47.280
And so the ranking that it
provides is much

131
00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.840
more conditional on
the specific query than just what

132
00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:52.000
is naively returned
by the retrieval system itself.

133
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.640
In the next lab, we'll
talk about query adapters.

134
00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:58.960
Query adapters are a way to
directly alter or augment the query

135
00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:02.160
embedding itself using
user feedback or other types of

136
00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:04.340
data to get better query results.
